WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.039
Welcome to Lecture 1 of the

2
00:00:02.104 --> 00:00:03.392
FrontierCS 2 course

3
00:00:03.392 --> 00:00:05.260
on machine-learning systems.

4
00:00:05.610 --> 00:00:08.171
Each task gives an AI agent real research

5
00:00:08.234 --> 00:00:10.232
code with one key part to write

6
00:00:10.295 --> 00:00:11.794
and a human reference to

7
00:00:11.857 --> 00:00:13.293
beat on every workload

8
00:00:13.293 --> 00:00:14.730
including hidden ones.

9
00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:18.386
This lecture covers six tasks.

10
00:00:18.386 --> 00:00:20.920
We start with SVG-EAR.

11
00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:23.923
A video diffusion model makes a clip

12
00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:25.740
from noise in fifty steps.

13
00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:28.981
An eighty-one-frame clip at seven twenty p has

14
00:00:29.044 --> 00:00:31.621
seventy-five thousand six hundred tokens

15
00:00:31.621 --> 00:00:32.753
and in attention

16
00:00:32.753 --> 00:00:34.890
every token looks at every other.

17
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:37.983
Sparse VideoGen2 clusters the tokens and

18
00:00:38.051 --> 00:00:40.520
computes only the pairs that matter.

19
00:00:40.870 --> 00:00:43.669
SVG-EAR, from ECCV 2026

20
00:00:43.669 --> 00:00:47.750
also estimates the pairs it skips.

21
00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:49.581
In the case study

22
00:00:49.581 --> 00:00:51.456
the example has one query

23
00:00:51.456 --> 00:00:53.610
and four keys in two clusters.

24
00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.760
Each key's weight is e to its score.

25
00:00:57.110 --> 00:01:00.230
In cluster A, both keys score one.

26
00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.300
In B, they score zero and two.

27
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:05.638
To estimate a skipped cluster

28
00:01:05.638 --> 00:01:07.890
give every key the average score.

29
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.840
For A, that is exact: five point four four.

30
00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:15.037
For B, it is also five point four four

31
00:01:15.037 --> 00:01:18.030
but the truth is eight point three nine.

32
00:01:18.610 --> 00:01:19.839
Pause and predict.

33
00:01:19.908 --> 00:01:21.751
You can compute one cluster

34
00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:23.391
and estimate the other.

35
00:01:23.391 --> 00:01:25.508
By estimated weight, they tie.

36
00:01:25.508 --> 00:01:27.010
Which do you compute?

37
00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:31.257
Compute B.

38
00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:33.140
A's estimate has no error

39
00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:35.302
so the output comes out exact.

40
00:01:35.371 --> 00:01:37.672
SVG-EAR ranks blocks by how wrong

41
00:01:37.742 --> 00:01:39.416
their estimate would be

42
00:01:39.416 --> 00:01:40.880
not by their weight.

43
00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:44.760
Our small layer has sixteen key clusters

44
00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:46.440
and thirty-two queries.

45
00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:48.396
Compute two of the sixteen

46
00:01:48.458 --> 00:01:50.003
blocks and drop the rest

47
00:01:50.065 --> 00:01:51.733
and the mean error is about

48
00:01:51.794 --> 00:01:53.030
zero point two four.

49
00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:54.608
Estimate the rest

50
00:01:54.608 --> 00:01:57.540
and it falls to zero point zero six seven.

51
00:01:57.890 --> 00:01:59.165
Rank by error too

52
00:01:59.165 --> 00:02:02.210
and it falls to zero point zero two eight.

53
00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.200
At every budget, the order holds.

54
00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.626
The task removes this attention

55
00:02:07.686 --> 00:02:09.353
layer from two video models

56
00:02:09.353 --> 00:02:11.140
and the agent writes it back.

57
00:02:11.490 --> 00:02:13.852
A pass is judged on twelve clips

58
00:02:13.852 --> 00:02:16.930
against the human reference, clip by clip.

59
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.539
Each clip must take at least

60
00:02:19.619 --> 00:02:21.474
four percent less time

61
00:02:21.555 --> 00:02:23.410
and lose almost nothing

62
00:02:23.491 --> 00:02:27.040
on three image scores: PSNR, SSIM and LPIPS.

63
00:02:27.390 --> 00:02:29.328
Twelve clips, four bars each

64
00:02:29.328 --> 00:02:30.665
forty-eight checks

65
00:02:30.665 --> 00:02:32.670
and a pass needs all of them.

66
00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:34.296
No model passed

67
00:02:34.361 --> 00:02:36.387
though thirteen of sixteen beat

68
00:02:36.452 --> 00:02:37.890
the reference's score.

69
00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:39.874
The best, GPT-6 Astra

70
00:02:39.949 --> 00:02:42.623
scored forty-eight point six against

71
00:02:42.697 --> 00:02:44.480
thirty-three point nine.

72
00:02:44.830 --> 00:02:45.897
Every fourth step

73
00:02:45.956 --> 00:02:47.616
it measures its own error on

74
00:02:47.676 --> 00:02:49.158
thirty-two probe queries

75
00:02:49.158 --> 00:02:51.470
and sets each head's budget from that.

76
00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:52.524
On average

77
00:02:52.524 --> 00:02:55.340
it beat the reference on all four measures.

78
00:02:55.690 --> 00:02:57.208
But on Wan clip three

79
00:02:57.277 --> 00:02:59.347
its PSNR fell zero point eight

80
00:02:59.416 --> 00:03:01.210
decibels short of the bar.

81
00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:04.200
It missed five checks, and failed.

82
00:03:04.780 --> 00:03:06.334
Astra also added a term

83
00:03:06.401 --> 00:03:07.820
the paper leaves out.

84
00:03:08.170 --> 00:03:10.066
The exponential bends upward

85
00:03:10.131 --> 00:03:12.288
so the estimate from the centroid

86
00:03:12.354 --> 00:03:13.530
is never too high.

87
00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.431
A second-order fix adds half the variance

88
00:03:16.493 --> 00:03:18.360
of the scores to the exponent.

89
00:03:18.710 --> 00:03:19.658
For cluster B

90
00:03:19.658 --> 00:03:21.892
that gives eight point nine six

91
00:03:21.892 --> 00:03:22.907
a little high

92
00:03:22.907 --> 00:03:25.750
but far closer than five point four four.

93
00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:30.090
Claude Opus 5.5 stayed closest to the paper.

94
00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:32.630
It passed all twelve speed checks

95
00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:35.400
but only thirty-two of forty-eight in all.

96
00:03:35.750 --> 00:03:37.430
Three things to take away.

97
00:03:37.780 --> 00:03:39.460
Estimate what you skip.

98
00:03:39.810 --> 00:03:42.210
Rank by the error of that estimate.

99
00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:44.501
And a pass needs every check

100
00:03:44.501 --> 00:03:45.840
not a good average.

101
00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:47.950
Next, Continuum.
